Description: hw/char/imx_serial: Leave the receive FIFO's last place free of a chardev
 The i.MX UART flags the character stored in the receive FIFO's 32nd
 place as an overrun (URXD_OVRRUN), and the model does too. A real line
 brings a character at a time, so the guest has emptied the FIFO long
 before it fills. A chardev, though, brings at once as many characters
 as imx_can_receive() allows, which is the whole free space, so any
 burst of 32 or more fills the last place. The guest's driver then drops
 the flagged character, or with the Chumby's 2.6.16 driver, in raw mode,
 passes on a 0 in its place: a byte lost or changed in every burst.
 .
 Add an "rx-headroom" property, off by default so other machines are
 unchanged. With it, a chardev is offered one place fewer than is free,
 so it never fills the last place. Nothing is lost by that: a chardev
 keeps what it hasn't delivered until the guest has read more. The
 i.MX21 SoC sets it on its four UARTs, so a socket or a pipe given to
 one with -serial can stream into the guest.
